@* Batch PROV-1, owner order 2 verbatim: "add a ? button on our frontier
   interface that gives provenance (i.e., sourced from openbible.com) or
   whatever."

   ONE small component, parameterized by the provenance it explains, usable
   on ANY frontier section and on the focus card itself -- the composability
   bar ("make sure all the new bits made for this are composable and
   reusable"), the same bar FrontierMetadataRow.razor already answers for
   time/place. It is NOT event-only markup, NOT section-only markup, and
   knows nothing about which surface mounted it: it takes a list of
   provenance ids plus a label, and renders what the registry says about
   them. THE KNOB TABLE is in batch-prov1-report.md; each parameter carries
   its own reason at the bottom of this file.

   NOT AN OVERLAY, ON PURPOSE (the §5 escape-hatch question, answered rather
   than labelled -- EVT-3's Q-1 lesson is that a label without the machinery
   is a finding, and the converse trap is inventing machinery to justify a
   label). This is an INLINE DISCLOSURE: the panel renders in normal flow
   directly beneath its own button, inside the section that owns it, and
   pushes the popover's subsequent content down. Nothing floats, nothing is
   positioned over anything, there is no focus trap, no backdrop and no
   scroll lock -- so §5's escape-hatch law, which governs surfaces a reader
   can get STUCK in, does not bind here, and saying so plainly is more
   honest than ceremonially registering an IEscapeHatch whose Invoke()
   could not carry this panel's parameters anyway. MapFocusHatch.cs's own
   header documents that exact conflict: every real IEscapeHatch in this app
   is constructed ONCE at ViewRegistrySetup.Build() time over DI singletons
   with a PARAMETERLESS Invoke(), and this panel's content is per-surface
   DATA varying per invocation -- the same shape that ruled MapFocusHatch
   out of the formal machinery.

   ESC still closes it and the button keeps focus -- not because a hatch
   contract demands it, but because a keyboard user must be able to undo any
   toggle they made.

   HOVER-KILL-1 IS LAW HERE. This opens on CLICK / ENTER / SPACE and on
   nothing else; there is no hover handler anywhere in this file.
   HOVER-KILL-1 retired hover boxes on event buttons for exactly the reason
   that binds again here -- the owner reads on touch devices, where "hover"
   is a tap that fires once and then cannot be dismissed. The hit target is
   a touch-friendly 44px minimum (app.css's own .popover-provenance-button
   rule), for the same reason. *@

@if (_ids.Count > 0)
{
    <span class="@RootClass" data-testid="@TestId">
        <button type="button"
                class="popover-provenance-button@(_open ? " is-open" : "")"
                data-testid="@ButtonTestId"
                aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
                aria-controls="@_panelId"
                aria-label="@ButtonLabel"
                title="@ButtonLabel"
                @onclick="Toggle"
                @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                @onkeydown="OnKeyDown"
                @onkeydown:stopPropagation="true">?</button>

        @if (_open)
        {
            <span class="popover-provenance-panel" id="@_panelId" role="note" data-testid="@PanelTestId">
                @foreach (var p in _resolved)
                {
                    @if (p.Status == Explore.ProvenanceStatus.RegistryUnavailable)
                    {
                        @* FIX ROUND 1 (review M-4): AN INFRASTRUCTURE FAULT,
                           SAID AS ONE. /api/sources did not load, so nothing
                           has been learned about this data -- it is not
                           "unrecognized," it is unchecked. Before this, one
                           dropped request rendered the red "Unrecognized
                           source" accusation on EVERY affordance on the
                           popover, telling the reader the atlas had
                           unattributed data and asking them to report a bug
                           that did not exist. The id is still named, because
                           it is the one true thing we can say. *@
                        <span class="popover-provenance-unavailable" data-testid="@UnavailableTestId">
                            The source list could not be loaded, so this cannot be named right now.
                            Its source id is &quot;@p.Id&quot;.
                        </span>
                    }
                    else if (p.Status == Explore.ProvenanceStatus.Unresolved)
                    {
                        @* THE FAIL-LOUD LAW, visibly. Never a silent blank,
                           never a fabricated label -- the offending id is
                           named so it can actually be fixed. A BLANK id
                           reaches here too (fix round 1, review H-1): the
                           four filters that used to drop it before
                           resolution are gone, so "we were handed nothing to
                           attribute this with" now SHOUTS instead of
                           rendering as no "?" at all. *@
                        <span class="popover-provenance-error" data-testid="@UnresolvedTestId">
                            @if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(p.Id))
                            {
                                @:This item was served with no source at all. Please report it.
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                @:Unrecognized source "@p.Id". Please report it.
                            }
                        </span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <span class="popover-provenance-entry" data-testid="@EntryTestId(p)">
                            <span class="popover-provenance-title">Sourced from @p.Title</span>
                            <span class="popover-provenance-what">@p.WhatItIs</span>
                            @if (ProvenanceResolver.ConfidenceNote(p.Confidence) is { } note)
                            {
                                @* Shown only when it is NOT the obvious
                                   default -- a CanonicalText claim and a
                                   Derived claim must not look alike. See
                                   ProvenanceResolver.ConfidenceNote for the
                                   rule and why "Imported" is the silent
                                   one. *@
                                <span class="popover-provenance-confidence" data-testid="@ConfidenceTestId(p)">@note</span>
                            }
                            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(p.Locator))
                            {
                                <span class="popover-provenance-locator" data-testid="@LocatorTestId(p)">Locator: @p.Locator</span>
                            }
                            <span class="popover-provenance-license">License: @p.License</span>
                        </span>
                    }
                }
            </span>
        }
    </span>
}

@code {
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE KNOBS. Four presentation/behavior knobs plus one data
    // dependency, and every one is genuinely varied by a live call site
    // today -- no knob is here "for later" (the composability bar cuts
    // both ways: reusable, but never speculative).
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>KNOB 1 -- WHAT IT EXPLAINS. The provenance ids behind this
    /// surface, in order. ONE id for a node or a single row; SEVERAL for a
    /// section genuinely drawing on several sources -- THE SMALL CATECHISM
    /// really does (`curated-catechism` + `concord-sc-overlap`, measured),
    /// and so does an "Mentioned in" section, and so does a verse belonging
    /// to both an imported and a hand-authored event -- and ALL of them
    /// render, because collapsing them is precisely the leper failure mode
    /// this affordance exists to make visible. An empty or null list renders NOTHING at all -- not an
    /// empty button, not a placeholder -- which is the same "return null
    /// when empty" conditional-presence discipline every section provider
    /// in this app already follows.
    ///
    /// <para>FIX ROUND 1 (review H-1): an empty LIST and a list holding a
    /// BLANK ID are different facts and now look different. Empty list =
    /// this surface has no attribution to make (honest absence). A blank id
    /// in the list = something claimed an attribution and handed over
    /// nothing, which renders the LOUD notice. The blank used to be
    /// filtered out here and in three other places, making them
    /// indistinguishable and the "never a silent blank" law false.</para></summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<string>? Provenance { get; set; }

    /// <summary>KNOB 2 -- IDENTITY. The stable test/CSS hook, exactly like
    /// <c>FrontierMetadataRow.TestId</c>. Button and panel derive theirs
    /// from it ("{TestId}-button", "{TestId}-panel"), so a fixture never
    /// has to guess a second name.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string TestId { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>KNOB 3 -- THE ACCESSIBLE NAME. "?" means nothing to a
    /// screen reader (or to a touch user with no hover), so every call site
    /// says what THIS "?" is about: "Sources for cross references",
    /// "Source for this event". Required rather than defaulted on purpose
    /// -- one generic default would be silently wrong on most surfaces, and
    /// a WRONG accessible name is worse than a missing one.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string ButtonLabel { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>KNOB 4 -- REGISTER. "header" (the default) sits on a
    /// section eyebrow beside a small-caps heading; "row" sits inline in a
    /// list row, one step smaller and tighter. A pure presentation knob
    /// with two live values today (see app.css's own
    /// <c>.popover-provenance--row</c> modifier). An unrecognized value
    /// falls back to the header register rather than throwing: a styling
    /// mistake is not a reason to break a reader's page.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Register { get; set; } = HeaderRegister;

    /// <summary>THE DATA DEPENDENCY (not a knob). The already-fetched
    /// registry, PASSED IN rather than injected, which is what keeps this
    /// component pure and synchronously renderable: the caller -- a section
    /// provider -- has already awaited <c>AtlasClient.Sources()</c>, which
    /// is <see cref="AsyncMemo{T}"/>-backed, so the whole app shares ONE
    /// fetch and this component can never start a second one (the sub-100ms
    /// frontier law). Null means the registry is not available -- either it
    /// has not resolved yet or its fetch failed -- and FIX ROUND 1 (review
    /// M-4) is that this no longer resolves every id as "unrecognized". It
    /// resolves them as <c>RegistryUnavailable</c>, which says the SOURCE
    /// LIST could not be loaded instead of accusing perfectly
    /// well-registered data of having no source on every affordance on the
    /// popover.</summary>
    [Parameter] public SourcesDocumentOut? Sources { get; set; }

    public const string HeaderRegister = "header";
    public const string RowRegister = "row";

    private bool _open;
    private readonly string _panelId = "prov-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N")[..8];
    private IReadOnlyList<string> _ids = Array.Empty<string>();
    private IReadOnlyList<ResolvedProvenance> _resolved = Array.Empty<ResolvedProvenance>();

    private string RootClass => Register == RowRegister
        ? "popover-provenance popover-provenance--row"
        : "popover-provenance";

    private string ButtonTestId => TestId + "-button";
    private string PanelTestId => TestId + "-panel";
    private string UnresolvedTestId => TestId + "-unresolved";
    private string UnavailableTestId => TestId + "-unavailable";
    private string EntryTestId(ResolvedProvenance p) => TestId + "-entry-" + p.Id;
    private string ConfidenceTestId(ResolvedProvenance p) => TestId + "-confidence-" + p.Id;
    private string LocatorTestId(ResolvedProvenance p) => TestId + "-locator-" + p.Id;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        // Resolution is a pure in-memory join over an ALREADY-fetched
        // document -- no fetch here, by construction (see
        // ProvenanceResolver's own doc comment and the sub-100ms frontier
        // law it cites). `_ids` is deduped the same way ResolveAll dedupes,
        // so the render gate and the rendered list can never disagree about
        // whether there is anything to say.
        //
        // FIX ROUND 1 (review H-1, HIGH): the `.Where(id =>
        // !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(id))` that used to sit here is GONE.
        // It, plus the identical filter in ResolveAll, plus two more in
        // PopoverSectionProviders, made an empty provenance render as NO "?"
        // AT ALL -- while this file's own comments claimed it rendered a
        // loud notice. THE GATE BELOW IS UNCHANGED and is where the honest
        // distinction lives: an empty LIST is still nothing at all
        // (conditional presence -- there is no attribution section here),
        // but a list containing a blank id is one entry, so the button
        // renders and the panel shouts. "Nothing to say" and "something
        // illegible to say" must not look the same.
        _ids = (Provenance ?? Array.Empty<string>()).Select(id => id ?? "").Distinct().ToList();
        _resolved = ProvenanceResolver.ResolveAll(Sources, _ids);
    }

    private void Toggle() => _open = !_open;

    private void OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // Enter/Space are already what a native <button> does; only Escape
        // needs handling, so a keyboard user can undo the toggle without
        // hunting for the button again.
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
        {
            _open = false;
        }
    }
}
